Actually, @AndersBroman, I might be wrong. "Our" section is different.

`channel_name = ws_strdup((const char*)str_network);` is within a for loop as shown below.
As many times "network" atribute name found, new memory allocation will occur. So I believe we shall do the memory freeing with that for loop. It seems the last "network" attribute will be used later in the code as `channel_name`.


```
            for (xmlAttrPtr attr = current_channel_node->properties; attr; attr = attr->next) {
                if (xmlStrcmp(attr->name, (const xmlChar*)"number") == 0) {
                    xmlChar* str_channel = xmlNodeListGetString(current_channel_node->doc, attr->children, 1);
                    if (str_channel != NULL) {
                        ws_strtou16((const char*)str_channel, NULL, &channel);
                        xmlFree(str_channel);
                    }
                } else if (xmlStrcmp(attr->name, (const xmlChar*)"type") == 0) {
                    xmlChar* str_type = xmlNodeListGetString(current_channel_node->doc, attr->children, 1);
                    if (str_type != NULL) {
                        pkt_encap = blf_get_xml_pkt_encap(str_type);
                        xmlFree(str_type);
                    }
                } else if (xmlStrcmp(attr->name, (const xmlChar*)"network") == 0) {
                    xmlChar* str_network = xmlNodeListGetString(current_channel_node->doc, attr->children, 1);
                    if (str_network != NULL) {
                        channel_name = ws_strdup((const char*)str_network);
                        xmlFree(str_network);
                    }
                }
            }
```

Okay, so the theoretical logic is clear, we know what the expectations are. But the current code does not reflect that. Coverity looks at the code without understanding the logic which was implemented. If you purely look at that for loop, it does allow channel_name to be reallocated if the attribute is "network" more than one time. There is nothing in the code to prevent multiple "network" attribute. So if the logic is that we are not allowed to have more than one "network" attribute then do not allow `ws_strdup()` more than 1 time either.

So then just add `if (channel_name == NULL)` there and it will only allow `channel_name = ws_strdup((const char*)str_network);` to be used once, and this will also fix the Coverity warning.

Additionally, this would also help to ignore any additional incorrectly present "network" attribute as a fail safe.


```
            char* channel_name = NULL;

            for (xmlAttrPtr attr = current_channel_node->properties; attr; attr = attr->next) {
                if (xmlStrcmp(attr->name, (const xmlChar*)"number") == 0) {
                    xmlChar* str_channel = xmlNodeListGetString(current_channel_node->doc, attr->children, 1);
                    if (str_channel != NULL) {
                        ws_strtou16((const char*)str_channel, NULL, &channel);
                        xmlFree(str_channel);
                    }
                } else if (xmlStrcmp(attr->name, (const xmlChar*)"type") == 0) {
                    xmlChar* str_type = xmlNodeListGetString(current_channel_node->doc, attr->children, 1);
                    if (str_type != NULL) {
                        pkt_encap = blf_get_xml_pkt_encap(str_type);
                        xmlFree(str_type);
                    }
                } else if (xmlStrcmp(attr->name, (const xmlChar*)"network") == 0) {
                    xmlChar* str_network = xmlNodeListGetString(current_channel_node->doc, attr->children, 1);
                    if (str_network != NULL) {
                        if (channel_name == NULL) // additional condition
                            channel_name = ws_strdup((const char*)str_network);
                        xmlFree(str_network);
                    }
                }
            }
```

